\@doanenote {216}
macro:->This
plot
reproduces
Figure
2
from
Hofmann,
Wickham,
and
Kafadar
(2017),
which
was
produced
with
the
R
package
\{lvplot\}
(Wickham
and
Hofmann
2025).
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {217}
macro:->In
order
to
incorporate
as
many
points
as
possible,
this
analysis
just
focuses
on
the
default
\{lvplot\}
settings
that
are
used
in
Figure~\ref
{fig-lv-default}
and
even
sinks
to
the
level
of
critiquing
the
specific
data
set
(and
non-linear
scales)
that
are
used
in
this
specific
plot.
Letter-value
plots
and
the
\{lvplot\}
package
are
capable
of
a
much
broader
range
of
results
than
the
specific
instance
that
we
focus
on
in
this
section.
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {218}
macro:->The
invention
of
letter
values
is
attributed
to
Tukey
(1977).
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {219}
macro:->Letter
values
are
an
interesting
data
summary
in
the
sense
that
each
letter
value
can
correspond
to
a
raw
data
value.
In
other
words,
letter
values
are
a
subset
of
the
original
raw
data
values,
chosen
to
represent
boundaries
beyond
which
a
certain
proportion
of
the
raw
data
values
lie.
\par
This
is
not
true
for
all
letter
values
because
a
letter
value
may
fall
between
two
raw
data
values.
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {220}
macro:->The
calculation
of
the
number
of
letter
values
to
display
is
based
on
the
uncertainty
in
the
estimate
of
the
letter
values
(assuming
Gaussian
data).
We
stop
once
a
95\%
confidence
interval
for
a
letter
value
overlaps
with
the
neighbouring
letter
value.
\par
This
calculation
is
relevant
only
for
the
default
letter-value
plot.
Variations
of
the
letter-value
plot
design
allow
for
different
calculations
of
the
number
of
letter
values
to
display.
\@endanenote 
\@doanenote {221}
macro:->This
point
is
only
relevant
to
the
default
letter-value
plot.
A
variation
of
the
letter-value
plot
design
involves
a
different
encoding
of
width
so
that
the
area
of
the
rectangle
reflects
the
proportion
of
the
data
between
the
letter
values.
\@endanenote 
